Found it. There's a stray brace in New-WorksheetFromHtml that closes the foreach loop one iteration too early — specifically, this line:

```powershell
            $ws = [PSCustomObject]@{
                Date      = $dt.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd")
                FetchedAt = (Get-Date).ToString("o")
                Source    = $Source
                Rows      = $decorated
		}}          ← ← ← this second } closes the foreach
```

The first } closes the hashtable literal; the second } closes the foreach ($target in $foundDates) loop. Everything after that point — Sync-WorksheetActualTimes, Sync-WorksheetJobHours, Add-WorksheetPlaceholders, Save-WeeklyWorksheet, Import-WorksheetJobsToJobLog, and $saved += ... — is therefore outside the loop and runs exactly once, against whatever $ws / $dt / $decorated are left over from the last iteration.

Why the log looks like this

Your log is telling you the exact bug:

```
New-WorksheetFromHtml: date 17-OCT-26 -> 14 data row(s)
New-WorksheetFromHtml: date 18-OCT-26 -> 13 data row(s)
...                                    ← parsing all 7 ✓
New-WorksheetFromHtml: date 23-OCT-26 -> 13 data row(s)

Weekly Worksheet: imported 7 date(s), 13 total row(s).
                                       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

DatesParsed = $foundDates.Count is 7 (correct — that variable was set before the loop). But TotalRows = ($saved | Measure-Object -Property Rows -Sum).Sum reports 13, because $saved only ever got one entry — the last iteration's, which had 13 rows. Seven dates × 13–14 rows each should sum to ~92. The mismatch between "7 dates parsed" and "13 total rows" is the tell.

Why the view is empty

Save-WeeklyWorksheet also only ran once, for 2026-10-23. So:

```
Work Tracking\Weekly Worksheets\2026-10-23.json   ← the only file that exists
```

Your wsDatePicker is on 2026-10-17 (today). Get-WeeklyWorksheet -Date 2026-10-17 looks for 2026-10-17.json, doesn't find it, returns $null, and Refresh-WeeklyWorksheetList renders the "No worksheet loaded" empty state. The parse worked; the save went to the wrong day.

Also silently lost: the Sync-WorksheetActualTimes / Add-WorksheetPlaceholders / Import-WorksheetJobsToJobLog passes only executed against 23-OCT-26's data, so today's PM hours never got folded into today's worksheet rows, and no pm.checklist placeholders were added for 10-17.

The fix

Change the double brace to a single brace:

```powershell
            $ws = [PSCustomObject]@{
                Date      = $dt.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd")
                FetchedAt = (Get-Date).ToString("o")
                Source    = $Source
                Rows      = $decorated
            }
```

That single character moves the sync/save/import block back inside the loop where it belongs. After this, each of the 7 dates will:

1. Sync actual times from PM checklists + worksheet row data
2. Update the standing WO cache
3. Sync job hours (reactive + WO)
4. Add NeedsAudit placeholders for any PM checklists without a worksheet row
5. Write its own YYYY-MM-DD.json
6. Import worksheet WOs into Jobs.json
7. Append to $saved with that day's row count

Your TotalRows should then jump from 13 to ~91, and 2026-10-17.json will exist so the view populates when you're on today's date.

After applying

Re-import the same HTML (Fetch or Paste — either is fine, Import-WorksheetJobsToJobLog is idempotent on (WO#, date) keys so no duplicate jobs will land in Jobs.json). You should see:

```
Weekly Worksheet: imported 7 date(s), 91 total row(s).
```

...or thereabouts, and today's rows will appear. The other six days will now be sitting in Work Tracking\Weekly Worksheets\ waiting when you flip the date picker forward.